;;; A rule's `:assert` splits a name that starts with `and` — **W22**, register
;;; row `W22/kw-and-in-assert` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`): a reserved word is
;;; a keyword only as a whole token (Q-M1e2.17).
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `kw-neq-in-assert.ein` with the relation `andz` in place of `neqw`: the same
;;; shape over another reserved word.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A keyword is a whole word: `grammar.md` § 1, fact 1, excludes a reserved
;;; word from `SYMBOL` only where a word boundary follows it (`neq` is not a
;;; `SYMBOL`; `neq_test` is), so `andz` is a name like any other. `r`
;;; concludes `(andz a)`: root holds `(andz a)` and `(p a)`, H is empty and R
;;; is {∅}, the answer of `kw-and-in-assert-twin.ein`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ────────────
;;;
;;; At `35186bc0` the parser read a reserved word as a prefix of a name, and
;;; this program answered wrong. The parser read `(andz ?x)` as the conjunction
;;; `(and z ?x)`, whose first conjunct `z` is no fact, so the run stopped at
;;; `r`'s first firing: every command that saturated exited 1 with *rule 'r':
;;; its :assert concludes z, which is not a fact — …*. (`ein solve FILE` at
;;; `35186bc0`, release and debug; every mode and `ein oracle` the same.) A
;;; keyword is a whole word now, at the boundary `grammar.md` § 1 states
;;; (Q-M1e2.17), and the program answers its twin's R.
;;;

(relation p Node :why "p holds of {?1}") (relation andz Node :why "andz holds of {?1}")
(p a)
(rule r () :match (p ?x) :assert (andz ?x) :why "p holds of {?x}, so andz holds of {?x}")
(query :goal (andz ?y))
